sec.p:
Although they were only observational studies, many recent papers have reported that fluid overload at the initiation of blood purification affects the survival outcomes. Body water is known to account for a larger percentage of the body weight in children than in adults. The percent fluid overload (%FO) is considered to be useful for the assessment of fluid overload.\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\begin{gathered} \% {\text{Fluid overload }}(\% {\text{FO}}){\text{ }}={\text{ }}({\text{fluid in}} - {\text{fluid out}})/{\text{PICU admission body weight}} \times {\text{1}}00{\text{ }}(\% ) \hfill \\ {\text{Fluid in}} - {\text{fluid out}}:{\text{ in}} - {\text{out balance before and after PICU admission}} \hfill \\ \end{gathered}$$\end{document}%Fluid overload(%FO)=(fluid in-fluid out)/PICU admission body weight×100(%)Fluid in-fluid out:in-out balance before and after PICU admission
